        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="true" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="false" heatedGarage="true" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" evChargingStation="false" outdoorGasAppliances="false" commonSpace="false" elevator="false" fitnessRoom="false" snowmeltSystem="false" unheatedGarage="false" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house is already fairly airtight, but I recommend that you air seal the following locations:



** Around the bulkhead in the back corner of your living room, in particular at the baseboards



** Around the new windows in the basement, to make sure that the gaps around the windows are filled as discussed during my visit



** As discussed, there is some leakage through the centre of the patio doors, where the two panels of the door meet



** I also recommend that you have an electronic damper added to your combustion air duct into your utility room. This must be installed by an HVAC contractor so that the damper opens and brings in fresh air when your boiler is firing, and closes when the boiler is not firing. 



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $70 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.3 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R15 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding.



The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings: By adding a minimum R15 to all of the above foundation exterior walls, which does not include the wall between your house and your garage, you could potentially save $540 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $4,595 in rebates from the DHERP. If you also add R15 to the wall between your house and your garage, you will be eligible for the full $5,000 rebate. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



If you are interested in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit, please contact me for more information. Projects must be completed by January 31st, 2022. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient oil boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE.



Savings: By replacing your current oil boiler with a 95% AFUE condensing propane boiler, you could potentially save $1,470 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 29.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). While updating your boiler, you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for up to $175 in rebates per pump (up to 2 per fiscal year) from EEIP. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program





COMBUSTION AIR



Your combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the boiler is blocked off. The combustion air pipe supplies fresh air to the boiler and should not be blocked off. You could have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler  is operating. Have a qualified heating contractor do this. </HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ECM Pumps

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency. These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. In addition, LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.8 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil boiler vents up a chimney and is susceptible to back drafting, as is your wood stove.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Wood Stove

Depending on how you use the wood stove in your living room, and the pellet stove you plan on installing in your basement, you will see savings in your energy bills. The value of these savings will depend on how and how often you use the stoves, but I modelled a rough estimate of savings of $250 for the wood stove, and $500 for the pellet stove.



There are rebates on pellet and wood stoves available through the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a bathroom fan in your upstairs bathroom

        - a clothes dryer



Your range hood is recirculating, and the downstair bathroom is only vented by the HRV. 



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.21 per liter 

Propane - $0.683 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



